VERDICT
Your logic is correct because it accurately finds all parts of the number to see if they add up, but it is partly correct overall because it checks too many numbers and might be a little slow for a strict interview.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well: You correctly used the modulo operator to find the pieces that divide evenly, and you safely kept a running total. Stopping the loop halfway was also a smart initial thought.

Is it correct?: Yes, it gives the right answer for all test cases. The main improvement is just cleaning up the final lines.

Fix: Instead of using an if-else block to return true or false, you can just return the answer to the math question directly. Change your last 5 lines to just this:

return sum == num;
Logical mistakes and edge cases: Your code handles the tricky case of 1 perfectly. For 1, half of 1 is 0. Your loop never runs, the sum stays 0, and 0 does not equal 1, so it correctly says false.

Time and space complexity: Time complexity is O(N) because the loop runs N/2 times. Space complexity is O(1) because you only use two simple variables for your math.

Why my approach works: A perfect number is the sum of its parts. Since no proper piece can be larger than half the number, checking every number up to that halfway point guarantees you will find all pieces. For example, with 28, you check 1 through 14 and successfully add 1, 2, 4, 7, and 14 to get 28.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Loop to Half (Your Approach)

Idea: Test every single number from 1 up to half of the input to see if it divides evenly, and add it to a running total.

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    bool checkPerfectNumber(int num) {
        int sum = 0;
        for(int i = 1; i <= num / 2; i++){
            if(num % i == 0){
                sum += i;
            }
        }
        return sum == num;
    }
};
Time and Space: Time is O(N) and Space is O(1).

What makes the next approach better: Finding one piece automatically gives you another one for free. If you know 2 divides 28, you instantly know 14 divides 28, so you can stop much earlier.

Approach 2: Square Root Pairs

Idea: Divisors always come in pairs. Instead of going up to half the number, you only need to go up to the square root of the number. When you find a piece, you add both it and its partner to the total.

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    bool checkPerfectNumber(int num) {
        if (num <= 1) {
            return false;
        }
        int sum = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i * i <= num; i++) {
            if (num % i == 0) {
                sum += i;
                if (i * i != num) {
                    sum += num / i;
                }
            }
        }
        return sum == num;
    }
};
Time and Space: Time is O(sqrt(N)) and Space is O(1).

What makes the next approach better: We can skip all the math if we just memorize the answers.

Approach 3: Hardcoded Math List

Idea: Mathematicians have proven there are only a handful of perfect numbers that fit inside a standard computer integer limit. We can just check if the number is one of those specific five numbers.

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    bool checkPerfectNumber(int num) {
        return num == 6 || num == 28 || num == 496 || num == 8128 || num == 33550336;
    }
};
Time and Space: Time is O(1) and Space is O(1).

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use?: You used Approach 1, the brute force loop up to the halfway point.

Which approach does an interviewer expect?: The interviewer expects to see Approach 2. It proves you understand how to use pairs to cut down the amount of work a computer has to do.

Common follow-up questions: "Can you do it without looping at all?" You can answer by mentioning Approach 3, explaining that the limited number of perfect numbers means you can just pre-calculate them.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER

The key insight: Divisors always come in pairs, so you only ever need to check up to the square root of a number.

My mistakes: Never write if a condition is true return true else return false. Just return the condition directly.

The pattern: Mathematical pairs and square root limits.

